nly."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2870cf14-3949-5d51-b043-39e85cf00600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\left(4\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567318} {:answer/id #uuid "33b09fc5-2518-54ed-ae1a-f60299abd95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\left(-8\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567322} {:answer/id #uuid "361b4247-8d09-5403-a5f5-461e97e4edb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\left(-4\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567323} {:answer/id #uuid "8f51bbec-a5b3-5e45-b804-48b8bfbefd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\left(10\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567330} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f70b02-e5a5-57ee-a081-974e3743d33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\left(-6\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33b09fc5-2518-54ed-ae1a-f60299abd95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\left(-8\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567322} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3508371-56bb-59d3-b9d2-7a40ede5a08e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "016f6e3c-38fb-537b-b003-822a8ff4c03d" :question/problem "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(-2)\\left((x+y)^{2}\\right)\\,i \\\\ & \\quad +((-2)\\left((y+z)^{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\ & \\quad +((-2)\\left((x+z)^{2}\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((-2)\\left((x+y)^{2}\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}((-2)\\left((y+z)^{2}\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}((-2)\\left((x+z)^{2}\\right))  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z)\\right)  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(4(x+y+z)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(4((-2)+(1)+(-1))\\right)  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(4\\cdot (-2)\\right)  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(-8\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "851f7b6c-6ef8-5fa1-93c1-2b75578d0591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567328} {:answer/id #uuid "85a7bf6a-008a-5ad4-bb57-98a16c1ea300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567329} {:answer/id #uuid "98f4ca2a-9f76-510d-99d8-13b8550e9b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567331} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a6ea42-172d-5293-9420-f6734616457c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567336} {:answer/id #uuid "fa85603b-2370-5ab3-b834-146df244880e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(4\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98f4ca2a-9f76-510d-99d8-13b8550e9b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567331} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ad85d6-9fc6-5588-8f2e-c1109fd32c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28c24fc2-984b-5c1a-852c-201e6e077ee6" :question/problem "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =2\\left({x}^{2}yz\\right)\\,i \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left({x}^{3}y\\right))\\,j \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\left({x}^{2}yz\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\left({x}^{3}y\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\left(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right))  \\\\ & =2\\left(2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) \\\\ & =2\\left(2(-1)(-1)(2)\\right. \\\\ & \\quad \\left. +(-1)^{3}\\right. \\\\ & \\quad \\left. +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-1}\\right)\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f91d37-da8d-54f8-ba9e-ee66408cf83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567314} {:answer/id #uuid "29aa8d63-92c4-5df0-8de4-ea7dc1c7e1c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567320} {:answer/id #uuid "4576db10-6579-5b11-9c02-feec57e9f666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567324} {:answer/id #uuid "5823b613-cd8d-5382-8ec9-9b675b6b7457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567326} {:answer/id #uuid "e382c867-db40-5fd0-b63d-5d586a0ccacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f91d37-da8d-54f8-ba9e-ee66408cf83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567314} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f4b39a-d9af-5934-9187-a0e2a66c417d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac507300-c358-5f81-a3a1-32ba16c5f8d7" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨- \\left(x - y\\right)^{3},\\,- 3 \\left(y + z\\right)^{3},\\,3 \\left(x + z\\right)^{3}⟩.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- \\left(x - y\\right)^{3}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 3 \\left(y + z\\right)^{3}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3 \\left(x + z\\right)^{3})  \\\\ & =(- 3 \\left(x - y\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(- 9 \\left(y + z\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(9 \\left(x + z\\right)^{2})  \\\\ & =- 3 \\left(x - y\\right)^{2} + 9 \\left(x + z\\right)^{2} - 9 \\left(y + z\\right)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1,-2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& (∇\\cdot F)(2,1,-2) \\\\ & =(- 3 \\left((2) - (1)\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(- 9 \\left((1) + (-2)\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(9 \\left((2) + (-2)\\right)^{2})  \\\\ & =(-3)+(-9)+(0)  \\\\ & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d10d140-d53c-5abc-bdc8-cba4649147ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567315} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3475f0-5b22-571b-a805-af303887d55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567321} {:answer/id #uuid "d060ccf1-e0a1-55a5-84f9-c904368bac0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567338} {:answer/id #uuid "f137330b-b37f-52d5-888d-d27c3ae8343d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567344} {:answer/id #uuid "fe390d00-7a59-5f61-a5c8-d39ee46d178e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f137330b-b37f-52d5-888d-d27c3ae8343d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567344} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1f2385-d3eb-5a18-8946-95b28e287c80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfb8af83-760c-5df6-bf98-3cc117b1cf57" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨\\left(2 x + y\\right)^{2},\\,\\left(- 3 y + 3 z\\right)^{2},\\,\\left(3 x + 2 z\\right)^{2}⟩.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,-1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(2 x + y\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(- 3 y + 3 z\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(3 x + 2 z\\right)^{2})  \\\\ & =(4 \\left(2 x + y\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(18 \\left(y - z\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(4 \\left(3 x + 2 z\\right))  \\\\ & =20 x + 22 y - 10 z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,-1,-2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& (∇\\cdot F)(-2,-1,-2) \\\\ & =(4 \\left(2 (-2) + (-1)\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(18 \\left((-1) - (-2)\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(4 \\left(3 (-2) + 2 (-2)\\right))  \\\\ & =(-20)+(18)+(-40)  \\\\ & =-42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4c7908-da86-57af-b238-66de9e09f459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(2\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567316} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7aeb16-636d-50c6-8819-32a432a19ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(4\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567325} {:answer/id #uuid "711aaab3-fdcf-5bc1-ac92-1df314216953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(6\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567327} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ac6e9-fcb6-5139-9cb0-e2b468832aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(3\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567339} {:answer/id #uuid "f80ecc3d-1bca-5c81-b3b3-1a574dfefce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d7aeb16-636d-50c6-8819-32a432a19ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(4\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567325} :answer-field/id #uuid "2448632d-78d0-53d8-9407-5709ed6299ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5d3b92e-3bda-59f9-9fb6-b7a712a6bbbf" :question/problem "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =2\\left(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\right)\\,i \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left(xy{e}^{z}\\right))\\,j \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left(z(x+1)\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\left(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\left(xy{e}^{z}\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\left(z(x+1)\\right))  \\\\ & =2\\left((y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+x{e}^{z}+(x+1)\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x{e}^{z}+x+1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& (∇\\cdot F)(1,1,0) \\\\ & =2\\left(1\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{0}+1+1\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(1+0+1+1+1\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(4\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182406d4-4bc8-5df8-ace2-66f9c6b4849d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x\\,i-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567387} {:answer/id #uuid "294128c7-1df2-5b09-8829-82717023955e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x^{2}\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567391} {:answer/id #uuid "a313364e-26e0-5771-98ca-5af94a43b3dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x\\,i+y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567409} {:answer/id #uuid "ac57a938-258d-5d53-85de-f301a8c83d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567412} {:answer/id #uuid "dc48f345-b9a2-54d7-bd7c-beb7b7b252c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac57a938-258d-5d53-85de-f301a8c83d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x\\,i-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567412} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fc60b33-efc4-51cf-9fc7-680c884a1737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13ee5558-9358-5fd4-aa9e-5a4aaf0794a3" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} - y$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x^{2} - y\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x^{2} - y\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=4 x\\,i-\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=4 x\\,i-\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2c5ab5-9bd2-5575-9f9d-2385a65a991a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y e^{2 x y}\\,i+2 x y e^{2 x y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567389} {:answer/id #uuid "389365a8-9909-5b3c-9041-efbb9d2b16c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,i+2 x^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567395} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce592c6-190f-5681-8c89-b125919c32f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(2 y + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,i+2 x e^{2 x y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567403} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3ec49e-687a-587b-b0f5-592e4b22f76a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x y e^{2 x y}\\,i+\\left(2 x + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567407} {:answer/id #uuid "a62534c0-4a89-57b4-b0e7-807af0ab9bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,i+\\left(2 x y + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a62534c0-4a89-57b4-b0e7-807af0ab9bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,i+\\left(2 x y + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567410} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ac93c0-1155-5ac5-8562-c8bba16e5f35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4775029e-af71-5606-8dc4-0e13a2685170" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y e^{2 x y}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(y e^{2 x y}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(y e^{2 x y}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,i+\\left(2 x y + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2 y^{2} e^{2 x y}\\,i+\\left(2 x y + 1\\right) e^{2 x y}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3455aaa3-3370-5e03-9e0e-752e60411548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567394} {:answer/id #uuid "6088f8a8-0d58-59f0-bffe-02f563f989b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567400} {:answer/id #uuid "93c8072c-1cd0-5f4c-a62e-ecc24a0c04ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,i+\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567408} {:answer/id #uuid "b59afd67-160f-5703-a278-5732289d994a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,i+\\frac{x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567413} {:answer/id #uuid "f393d274-a4a6-555f-9a3b-f5e5d7e2f0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3455aaa3-3370-5e03-9e0e-752e60411548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567394} :answer-field/id #uuid "3327c7c9-64d8-5998-a94b-0cd19fd73f12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "582c9ed0-4009-57e8-8025-a9c13aed2404" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\,i+\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de33882-0105-5da1-9a1e-11d179acc82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{2 y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567397} {:answer/id #uuid "684f6afa-7311-5d22-8197-c360216ca277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2 \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2 \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567401} {:answer/id #uuid "89df3c95-7c98-5538-bf3f-a173c8711caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567406} {:answer/id #uuid "ac528899-6d41-5386-a08d-7eb7d7b0fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\,i+2 y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567411} {:answer/id #uuid "bdbe0de1-f90d-5853-9c5d-026aaa3e44d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\,i+x \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89df3c95-7c98-5538-bf3f-a173c8711caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567406} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fa3817d-0743-5e69-a09a-83468d3649cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c9f1098-3929-5d29-8dbb-f64332ca5f8e" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 5}}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0570f3c2-cc2e-59b7-a737-d40139e89488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i-\\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567385} {:answer/id #uuid "266b123a-6281-52c5-a68d-cf1178c56132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+2 \\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567390} {:answer/id #uuid "298afdac-97ae-58e8-ba64-46c33132c338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567392} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbb3d08-9baf-5ccc-b8ce-a4a1951b2acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567399} {:answer/id #uuid "dd099bca-9ef2-5947-b854-6017c4d211b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i-2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bbb3d08-9baf-5ccc-b8ce-a4a1951b2acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567399} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3cab4f9-a582-59e0-8a08-d983b4046d24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9551d88-6838-5cf9-a7f6-e296594aafb8" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &=\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,i+2 \\cos{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ee2f7c-247b-52aa-9772-dd5afaed8cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,(6y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567464} {:answer/id #uuid "768baa50-a05a-5a93-8a7d-c7eafc56d215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,(7y+z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567472} {:answer/id #uuid "78e89c2c-64e1-5070-85f7-d9057064fd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567474} {:answer/id #uuid "a7661633-5385-5f1b-bf44-36c47f194c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}^{y-2z+2}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567481} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4f393a-a837-557c-8ec1-f57355dc6c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}^{y-2z+2}\\,(6y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78e89c2c-64e1-5070-85f7-d9057064fd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567474} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ffb8ed-a3fc-5061-963c-f9e84dcfaa17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "446721e6-e784-5554-b75e-8df7d632fd3e" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $x=y-2z+2$ and $x=-y^{2}-z^{2}+6$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(3xy^{2})\\,i+(xz)\\,j+(y)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & =3y^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & =3y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}-1\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ y-2z+2\\le x\\le -y^{2}-z^{2}+6\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z+2}^{-y^{2}-z^{2}+6}\\,3y^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124580ce-b588-5d3e-9e6a-c572d58acbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567457} {:answer/id #uuid "87a415a9-7010-5dbc-ab45-43faaef320c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,(18xz^{2}+8z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567475} {:answer/id #uuid "944d0d6d-8299-5ec4-88d6-8f32d284cab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567477} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8d52c-c2fc-57b1-943f-48958d8dfaca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567483} {:answer/id #uuid "ff36e153-7038-5984-9043-4c16de9c0b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,(18xz^{2}+8z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf8d52c-c2fc-57b1-943f-48958d8dfaca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567483} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33f842f-ed58-50c9-9066-163f83156bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fc3af64-5048-55c7-b8cf-0b264930b564" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1$, $y=1$, $z=0$, $z=2$, and the surfaces $x=y-z+3$ and $x=y^{2}+4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6)\\,i+(6xz^{3})\\,j+(8xz)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xz^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8xz) \\\\  & =0+0+8x \\\\  & =8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}8x\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}-1\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 2 \\\\ y-z+3\\le x\\le y^{2}+4\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}8x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-z+3}^{y^{2}+4}\\,8x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25474019-29f2-5d23-bd25-c70f93ba8fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,(8x+8y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567461} {:answer/id #uuid "2df2f35a-fd7f-58bd-9a38-5d67fd7e04a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4}^{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}\\,(10xy+24y^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567463} {:answer/id #uuid "51466662-d135-556e-87ab-060e7701ea44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567469} {:answer/id #uuid "75383faf-100a-5fce-9614-49109b1eda2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,(10xy+24y^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567471} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb607ac-2c00-52b0-a185-aea08347a312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51466662-d135-556e-87ab-060e7701ea44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567469} :answer-field/id #uuid "99fbac14-39f5-5ce3-9082-f2eb9f660e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d6274c-6dfe-5f3c-a4fd-5791e674d566" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=\\frac{1}{2}$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $y=x^{2}+z^{2}+2$ and $y=4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(8y^{3}-8z)\\,i+(8z)\\,j+(18xyz)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8y^{3}-8z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(18xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+18xy \\\\  & =18xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}18xy\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ x^{2}+z^{2}+2\\le y\\le 4\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}18xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+z^{2}+2}^{4}\\,18xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2419d35f-c467-510