ficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9be79e17-8b58-5c1b-806e-44da9409c071" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, $y=0$, $y=1$, and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=x^{2}+y+2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6x+6y)\\,i+(-14y)\\,j+(6x^{2}z)\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x+6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-14y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6x^{2}z) \\\\  & =6+(-14)+6x^{2} \\\\  & =6x^{2}-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ y\\le z\\le x^{2}+y+2\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{x^{2}+y+2}\\,(6x^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f53cd9-452a-5b7a-90b0-7cf01f8c51e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{6}\\,(6z^{3}-20)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567465} {:answer/id #uuid "5f378c1e-cb4b-520c-b85b-e9642eaa693a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(6z^{3}-20)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567470} {:answer/id #uuid "b4517b2b-d5ce-589e-b0b6-535bfba07d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567482} {:answer/id #uuid "f342e2b8-7df2-5134-a17a-98c7501cee4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,6z^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567488} {:answer/id #uuid "fa44c4cb-442e-5ee1-a900-ce805cf6055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa44c4cb-442e-5ee1-a900-ce805cf6055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567489} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cfce2e-64df-5654-aecd-ec39a7902c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be69cf6b-98de-5d36-ac17-56f847206dd5" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $z=0$, $z=1$, and the surfaces $y=x^{2}+2$ and $y=x-z+6$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(6z)\\,i+(-26y)\\,j+(6z^{3})\\,k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-26y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6z^{3}) \\\\  & =0+(-26)+18z^{2} \\\\  & =18z^{2}-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{(x,y,z):\\begin{array}{l}0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0\\le z\\le 1 \\\\ x^{2}+2\\le y\\le x-z+6\\end{array}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&{∭}_{R}(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x^{2}+2}^{x-z+6}\\,(18z^{2}-26)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2440da94-c49c-5b97-93a3-f1f305aaf546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571599} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfdfa0c-df5d-578c-9e5a-030e1e19fe81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571679} {:answer/id #uuid "67441758-f1aa-545c-a9ad-7fa7f49bf8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571715} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5ed5a3-55f9-5465-8515-fdd310345cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571970} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4a6f37-6e13-59de-b1f0-4ae8dde2510a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2440da94-c49c-5b97-93a3-f1f305aaf546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571599} :answer-field/id #uuid "5afb038d-0b31-5d55-8f54-c760388f6c4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87ece511-fd94-56a1-8fa2-055336c9156b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48848" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}$ at the point where $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+24.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 6x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting  $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}}\\right)|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{\\sqrt{3(-2)^{2}+24}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "557cd32f-67a1-5f7d-8007-8e8dc4e07e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})}^{2}(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571691} {:answer/id #uuid "82e6a967-6c7d-5d88-8afe-9d6fff949b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})(3{x}^{4}-6{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571760} {:answer/id #uuid "baf7a497-5f73-5773-889e-5e6f697822ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571872} {:answer/id #uuid "caaa2a22-48c5-53fe-92ea-da472c888e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{2})(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571901} {:answer/id #uuid "df3cad62-492d-5cb0-b710-42db57802071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}){(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baf7a497-5f73-5773-889e-5e6f697822ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571872} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbfbd138-787c-5944-9a48-89b79ecd75b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d6e7296-3732-5370-89a1-2dbb983a355c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71951" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096b9b79-42b4-5435-8d17-15938cf8083f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+4x}{\\sqrt{x-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571556} {:answer/id #uuid "528cbd11-0bdd-5861-899e-cfe78186526c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571688} {:answer/id #uuid "c895d3ba-c44d-57f2-8b2f-97e98d154d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1-2x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571895} {:answer/id #uuid "de570552-69e0-5331-86f3-79143ee949ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2+4x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571936} {:answer/id #uuid "f88f7c0f-9d24-5617-aa25-c79abffb93e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-4x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f88f7c0f-9d24-5617-aa25-c79abffb93e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-4x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571966} :answer-field/id #uuid "c878ff99-495e-5613-8683-22e308482f13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5c85892-5c29-5ec5-80d2-d47ed89d83b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-107298" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x-{x}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=x-{x}^{2}$ and $f(g(x))=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{g}}=-4{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =-4({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=\\frac{2}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(x-{x}^{2})^{′}=1-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (1-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2-4x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88aef948-a3e2-5b2a-a0db-15016ea2ff80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88aef948-a3e2-5b2a-a0db-15016ea2ff80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573000} :answer-field/id #uuid "22e5c6b4-ff57-5524-b47e-5c643c0d161c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9dc5ff-4edd-506a-a106-6d4ea74be127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9dc5ff-4edd-506a-a106-6d4ea74be127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572956} :answer-field/id #uuid "7151b114-fe10-5f5b-a6b1-fb98bfff8174" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 175921865731